      "id": 1,
      "text": "Colma was founded as a necropolis in 1924, becoming the first town in California built primarily for cemeteries.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Colma%2C_California",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s original name was Lawndale, but a 1941 name change restored Colma after a nearby city already claimed the Lawndale title.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Colma%2C_California",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The San Francisco and San Jose railroad ran through Colma in 1862, and a depot built 1863‑65 now sits in the Colma Historical Park.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15,
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Colma%2C_California",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Colma’s 100th‑anniversary film, produced by Kingston Media, premiered in 2024 to celebrate a century of quiet history.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
